# Exercise 3 - Simple neural network (iris)

A neural network built from scratch (no libraries besides `random`, `math`
and `csv`) that classifies iris flowers by species from their 4
measurements. Two files:

  - neuro_networks_algorithm.py - the network itself
  - experiments.py              - sweeps settings and logs results to CSV

## neuro_networks_algorithm.py

  - PART 1 - load the iris data            (load_flowers)
  - PART 2 - prepare the data               (species_to_true_answer, prepare_flowers)
                                             shuffle, split into training/testing, species -> [0, 1, 0]
  - PART 3 - create the network             (create_layer, create_network)
                                             random starting weights and biases
  - PART 4 - forward pass                   (calculate_totals, relu, softmax, forward_pass)
                                             measurements -> hidden 1 -> hidden 2 -> output scores -> probabilities
  - PART 5 - loss                           (calculate_loss)
                                             -ln(probability the network gave the correct species)
  - PART 6 - backward pass                  (calculate_layer_derivatives, calculate_hidden_blames, backward_pass)
                                             the derivative of every weight and bias, for one flower
  - PART 7 - update                         (update_network)
                                             new = old - learning rate x derivative
  - PART 8 - training loop                  (train_network)
                                             repeats forward pass -> loss -> backward pass -> update, per epoch
  - PART 9 - testing                        (test_network)
                                             forward pass only, returns accuracy

## experiments.py

Imports the functions above and runs every combination of learning rate,
epoch count, hidden-layer size and train/test split, several times each
(different seed per run), then averages the results.

  - PART 1 - run one experiment             (run_experiment)
                                             prepare, create, train and test one network
  - PART 2 - run every combination          (run_all_experiments)
                                             loops over all settings, averages NUMBER_OF_RUNS runs per combination
  - PART 3 - save to CSV                    (save_csv, main)
                                             writes results.csv and loss_curves.csv

Settings to try are changed at the top of the file: LEARNING_RATES, EPOCHS,
HIDDEN_NEURONS, TRAIN_SHARES, NUMBER_OF_RUNS.

Run with:

    python3 experiments.py

Output:
  - results.csv     - one row per settings combination (averaged accuracy,
                       best/worst accuracy, final loss, time taken)
  - loss_curves.csv - one row per epoch per combination, for plotting how
                       loss drops during training
